  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Apartment pool rules exist for two reasons: to keep everyone safe when there is usually no lifeguard, and to keep a shared space pleasant for dozens of households. Most communities cover the same basics, like set hours, guest limits, no glass and proper swimwear, and good etiquette fills in the rest. Here is what to expect, how to be the neighbor people are glad to see at the pool, and the safety habits that matter most.</p>

<h2>Common apartment pool rules</h2>
<p>Every property writes its own policy, so read the posted sign and your lease addendum. These are the rules you will see most often:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hours of operation.</strong> Pools typically open in the morning and close in the evening. After-hours swimming is prohibited and riskier because of lower light and nobody around to help.</li>
<li><strong>Guest limits.</strong> Many communities allow a small number of guests per unit, and the resident must stay with them.</li>
<li><strong>Access fobs or wristbands.</strong> Some properties track pool access, and lending your fob to non-residents can get your access revoked.</li>
<li><strong>No lifeguard on duty.</strong> Most apartment pools are swim-at-your-own-risk.</li>
<li><strong>Swimwear only.</strong> Cotton clothes, jeans and street shorts shed fibers and clog filters.</li>
<li><strong>No glass.</strong> One broken bottle can shut the pool down for cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>No smoking or vaping</strong> in the pool area at many communities.</li>
<li><strong>Swim diapers</strong> for children who are not potty trained.</li>
<li><strong>Age rules.</strong> Kids under a certain age often must be accompanied by an adult resident.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pool operations are also regulated. In Texas, public and semi-public pools, which typically include apartment pools, are subject to state health and safety rules and often local health department inspections. If you have concerns about how a pool is maintained, the leasing office is the first call, and your city or county health department is the next.</p>

<h2>Pool etiquette: how to be a good neighbor</h2>
<p>Rules cover the basics. Etiquette is what makes the pool a place people actually want to be.</p>
<h3>Keep the noise reasonable</h3>
<p>Music through earbuds or at a low volume is fine; a speaker blasting across the deck is not. Remember that some units overlook the pool, and people may be working or sleeping.</p>
<h3>Do not save chairs you are not using</h3>
<p>Draping towels over four loungers and leaving for two hours is a fast way to annoy everyone. On busy weekends, keep your footprint small and rotate out if the pool is crowded.</p>
<h3>Rinse off first</h3>
<p>A quick shower removes sunscreen, sweat and lotions, which helps keep the water clearer and reduces chemical demand.</p>
<h3>Clean up completely</h3>
<p>Take your trash, food wrappers and toys with you. Crumbs attract ants and birds, and forgotten floats end up in the lost and found or the dumpster.</p>
<h3>Respect personal space</h3>
<p>Keep cannonballs, splashing and pool games away from people who are relaxing, especially in small pools.</p>

<h2>What to pack for a smooth pool day</h2>
<p>Being prepared makes it easy to follow the rules. Bring towels, sunscreen, a hat, water in a non-glass bottle, your access fob and, for kids, swim diapers and a properly fitted life jacket if they are not strong swimmers. A roomy, water-resistant [[amz:pool_bag|pool tote]] keeps it all together and wipes clean. A small [[amz:first_aid|first aid kit]] is handy for scraped knees and stubbed toes on the deck.</p>
<!--products:pool_bag,first_aid|Pack these for the community pool-->

<h2>Safety first: no lifeguard means you are the lifeguard</h2>
<p>Drowning can happen quickly and quietly. Whether you are a parent or just someone at the pool, these habits matter:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Actively watch children.</strong> Designate one adult as the "water watcher" with no phone. Floaties and noodles are not safety devices.</li>
<li><strong>Never swim alone,</strong> especially after hours or if you have any medical condition.</li>
<li><strong>Know the emergency gear.</strong> Find the life ring, reaching pole, emergency phone and posted address before you need them.</li>
<li><strong>Walk, do not run.</strong> Wet decks are slippery.</li>
<li><strong>No diving in shallow water.</strong> Respect depth markers and "no diving" signs.</li>
<li><strong>Go easy on alcohol.</strong> It impairs judgment and coordination in and around water.</li>
<li><strong>Report hazards immediately.</strong> A gate that does not self-close and latch, cloudy water, broken tiles or missing drain covers should be reported right away.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Save your property's emergency maintenance number in your phone under "Pool / Maintenance." If a gate is broken or the water looks off on a weekend, you can report it in seconds.</p></div>
<p>Learning CPR is one of the most useful things any adult can do. Organizations like the American Red Cross and local hospitals often offer classes.</p>

<h2>Apartment pool vs. a pool of your own</h2>
<p>Many of our readers rent while they save for or plan a home build, and a backyard pool is often on the wish list. It is worth knowing what changes when the pool is yours:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Apartment pool</th><th>Private home pool</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Management handles chemicals, filters and repairs</td><td>You handle it or pay a service</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safety barriers</td><td>Community provides fencing and gates</td><td>You must meet local barrier and fencing codes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Covered by the property</td><td>Often means reviewing liability coverage with your insurer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Built into rent</td><td>Construction, utilities and upkeep add up</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Pool barriers and fencing are governed by building codes and local amendments, so check with your permitting office before you design one. Our guide on <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">navigating Texas building codes</a> is a good starting point, and <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-house-in-texas/">how much it costs to build a house in Texas</a> helps you see where a pool fits in the budget.</p>

<h2>Looking for an apartment with a great pool?</h2>
<p>When you tour, look past the photos. Is the water clear? Do the gates self-close? Are rules posted and enforced? Is the deck clean? A well-run pool is usually a sign of a well-run community. If you are apartment hunting in Houston, our roundup of <a href="/houstons-hottest-apartments-your-guide-to-stylish-city-living/">Houston's hottest apartments</a> and our guide to <a href="/apartments-for-families-finding-kid-friendly-features/">kid-friendly apartment features</a> can help.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
